<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-10-27.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="054641"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#054641" label="[05:46:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">urgh, not openssl problems again</span></div><div class="line" id="054651"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#054651" label="[05:46:51]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm working on getting bearssl in a state suitable for bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="054658"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#054658" label="[05:46:58]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">should be ready soonish</span></div><div class="line" id="112244"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#112244" label="[11:22:44]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Well, after Linux openssl would do</span></div><div class="line" id="112304"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#112304" label="[11:23:04]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; But before that, it would be nice to have something small</span></div><div class="line" id="120650"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#120650" label="[12:06:50]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well yeah</span></div><div class="line" id="120708"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#120708" label="[12:07:08]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">openssl is also just much more complicated lol</span></div><div class="line" id="134338"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#134338" label="[13:43:38]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; I wonder if bearssl is buildable with tcc...</span></div><div class="line" id="134402"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#134402" label="[13:44:02]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Though we have no network before Linux anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="193000"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#193000" label="[19:30:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: have you seen this PR <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/pull/57">https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/pull/57</a>  I was just made aware of it today</span></div><div class="line" id="200416"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#200416" label="[20:04:16]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bearssl at least with upstream tcc builds fine, including tests</span></div><div class="line" id="200456"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200456" label="[20:04:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, we have upstream tcc too</span></div><div class="line" id="200502"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200502" label="[20:05:02]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(in live-bootstrap)</span></div><div class="line" id="222339"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#222339" label="[22:23:39]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm trying to get the AMD64 part of the stage0-posix project on my system, but I keep running into errors. When I try to investigate the executable, I get the file type: &quot;/tmp/M0: ELF 64-bit LSB executable, x86-64, version 1 (GNU/Linux), can't read elf program headers at 1073741824, no section header&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="222449"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#222449" label="[22:24:49]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I used hex2 to compile it from the `AMD64` submodule, any ideas?</span></div><div class="line" id="223624"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223624" label="[22:36:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">notgull: hi</span></div><div class="line" id="223626"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223626" label="[22:36:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">did you run hex2 manually?</span></div><div class="line" id="223638"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223638" label="[22:36:38]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">my guess would be wrong optoins then...</span></div><div class="line" id="223645"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223645" label="[22:36:45]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I mean, here's the commands I ran:</span></div><div class="line" id="223647"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223647" label="[22:36:47]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">cat ../AMD64/ELF-amd64.hex2 ../AMD64/M0_AMD64.hex2 &gt; /tmp/M0.hex2</span></div><div class="line" id="223655"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223655" label="[22:36:55]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">./bin/hex2 -f /tmp/M0.hex2 -o /tmp/M0</span></div><div class="line" id="223701"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223701" label="[22:37:01]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Is there supposed to be another options on hex2?</span></div><div class="line" id="223716"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223716" label="[22:37:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, we need to specify --little-endian --architecture amd64</span></div><div class="line" id="223720"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223720" label="[22:37:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and I think --base-address</span></div><div class="line" id="223740"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223740" label="[22:37:40]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">kaem scripts know about these options</span></div><div class="line" id="223800"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223800" label="[22:38:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(or if you run make test-amd64 from stage0-posix directory)</span></div><div class="line" id="223813"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223813" label="[22:38:13]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It looks like specifying the first two options made it compile</span></div><div class="line" id="223816"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223816" label="[22:38:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(which just kicks of ./bootstrap-seeds/POSIX/AMD64/kaem-optional-seed</span></div><div class="line" id="223819"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223819" label="[22:38:19]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Oh, let me run that manually</span></div><div class="line" id="223826"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223826" label="[22:38:26]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, it will probably crash</span></div><div class="line" id="223829"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223829" label="[22:38:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">without base-address</span></div><div class="line" id="223835"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223835" label="[22:38:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as then base-address is assumed to be 0</span></div><div class="line" id="223837"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223837" label="[22:38:37]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: You would be correct</span></div><div class="line" id="223853"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223853" label="[22:38:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">most other reasonable numbers would work</span></div><div class="line" id="223911"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223911" label="[22:39:11]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I guess as long as it's outside reserved ranges, something over 1MiB would probably work</span></div><div class="line" id="223912"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223912" label="[22:39:12]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ok now I have the executable I wanted, thanks!</span></div><div class="line" id="223939"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223939" label="[22:39:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stage-posix-amd64 uses 0x00600000</span></div><div class="line" id="223941"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223941" label="[22:39:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for base address</span></div><div class="line" id="223949"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223949" label="[22:39:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but it is kind of arbitrary choice</span></div><div class="line" id="223952"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223952" label="[22:39:52]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Mmm, I imagine it's largely irrelevant?</span></div><div class="line" id="224008"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224008" label="[22:40:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, largely irrelevant</span></div><div class="line" id="224034"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224034" label="[22:40:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nothing should change as long as Linux accepts that base address in elf header</span></div><div class="line" id="224044"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224044" label="[22:40:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in fact almost all stage-posix-amd64 binaries are position independent</span></div><div class="line" id="224054"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224054" label="[22:40:54]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nice nic3</span></div><div class="line" id="224054"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224054" label="[22:40:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(I think kaem-optional-seed is the only exception for now_</span></div><div class="line" id="224109"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224109" label="[22:41:09]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Anyhow what I'm trying to do is get cc_amd64 working</span></div><div class="line" id="224114"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224114" label="[22:41:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but even for PIE binaries, Linux elf loader doesn't like if base address is 0</span></div><div class="line" id="224115"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224115" label="[22:41:15]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I wanted to see what its limits are</span></div><div class="line" id="224125"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224125" label="[22:41:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, cc_amd64 is fairly limited</span></div><div class="line" id="224126"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224126" label="[22:41:26]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">So I know what I can and can't add to M2-Planet</span></div><div class="line" id="224142"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224142" label="[22:41:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, basically looking at M2-Planet would give you a good idea what is supported</span></div><div class="line" id="224202"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224202" label="[22:42:02]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for structs, I think you are limited to struct pointers</span></div><div class="line" id="224202"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224202" label="[22:42:02]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Mmmm, I take it for loops are not?</span></div><div class="line" id="224207"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224207" label="[22:42:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">loops are fine</span></div><div class="line" id="224220"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224220" label="[22:42:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for while and do while should work</span></div><div class="line" id="224221"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224221" label="[22:42:21]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I see</span></div><div class="line" id="224239"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224239" label="[22:42:39]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, for loops can't do for (int i = 0; ...)</span></div><div class="line" id="224247"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224247" label="[22:42:47]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">My assumption is that &quot;unsigned char&quot; and any other &quot;unsigned [X]&quot; types aren't supported either, right?</span></div><div class="line" id="224248"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224248" label="[22:42:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you need to declare int i = 0 before for loop</span></div><div class="line" id="224251"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224251" label="[22:42:51]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(In M2-Planet as well)</span></div><div class="line" id="224301"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224301" label="[22:43:01]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think they are</span></div><div class="line" id="224314"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224314" label="[22:43:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">probably better support in M2-Planet than in cc_x86</span></div><div class="line" id="224336"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224336" label="[22:43:36]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if you see, M2-Planet assembly outputs, i.e. emit_out functions</span></div><div class="line" id="224348"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224348" label="[22:43:48]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm trying to compile QBE with M2-Planet, and the error I'm getting is this</span></div><div class="line" id="224349"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224349" label="[22:43:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">then you can see lots of signed vs unsiged cases</span></div><div class="line" id="224353"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224353" label="[22:43:53]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;/home/jtnunley/Projects/c9x/qbe/all.h:11:ERROR in typedef statement&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="224408"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224408" label="[22:44:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">e.g. <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/blob/master/cc_core.c#L1319">https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/blob/master/cc_core.c#L1319</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="224411"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224411" label="[22:44:11]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That statement is: typedef unsigned char uchar;</span></div><div class="line" id="224413"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224413" label="[22:44:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">arithmetic_recursion(additive_expr_a, &quot;ADD R0 R1 R0\n&quot;, &quot;ADDU R0 R1 R0\n&quot;, &quot;+&quot;, additive_expr_stub_b);</span></div><div class="line" id="224428"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224428" label="[22:44:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, typedef might not be smart enough here</span></div><div class="line" id="224438"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224438" label="[22:44:38]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ah, I see</span></div><div class="line" id="224443"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224443" label="[22:44:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">or maybe type name, let me check</span></div><div class="line" id="224456"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224456" label="[22:44:56]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">types are here <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/blob/master/cc_types.c">https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Planet/blob/master/cc_types.c</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="224510"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224510" label="[22:45:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(for M2-Planet at least, cc_x86 supports less)</span></div><div class="line" id="224513"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224513" label="[22:45:13]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I looked there, I see &quot;unsigned&quot; but not &quot;unsigned char&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="224522"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224522" label="[22:45:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, I wouldn't use cc_x86 for anything other than M2-Planet</span></div><div class="line" id="224532"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224532" label="[22:45:32]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">So I wanted to take a crack at rewriting that file a bit, but I needed to know what cc_x86 supporte</span></div><div class="line" id="224535"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224535" label="[22:45:35]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">supported*</span></div><div class="line" id="224644"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224644" label="[22:46:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, maybe the problem is that only 1 word types are supported</span></div><div class="line" id="224659"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224659" label="[22:46:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, looks like indeed there is no &quot;unsingned char&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="224709"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224709" label="[22:47:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">only unsinged for &quot;unsigned int&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="224800"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224800" label="[22:48:00]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah that's what I figured</span></div><div class="line" id="224813"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224813" label="[22:48:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is a fairly simple compiler...</span></div><div class="line" id="224830"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224830" label="[22:48:30]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I saw, yeah</span></div><div class="line" id="224840"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224840" label="[22:48:40]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I was investigating if it's feasible to add that in the current compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="224847"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#224847" label="[22:48:47]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Make it so `type_name()` can handle it</span></div><div class="line" id="224951"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#224951" label="[22:49:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, you can always at least look ahead for the further tokens</span></div><div class="line" id="225003"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225003" label="[22:50:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">global_token-&gt;next and global_token-&gt;next-&gt;next, etc...</span></div><div class="line" id="225016"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225016" label="[22:50:16]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That's what I figured would work</span></div><div class="line" id="225020"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225020" label="[22:50:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so perhaps some kind of longest match</span></div><div class="line" id="225103"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225103" label="[22:51:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and as for cc_x86, it basically has no support for pre-processor</span></div><div class="line" id="225114"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225114" label="[22:51:14]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah, cc_x86 is like</span></div><div class="line" id="225119"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225119" label="[22:51:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">no more advanced stuff like switch</span></div><div class="line" id="225121"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225121" label="[22:51:21]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Basically just enough of C</span></div><div class="line" id="225127"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225127" label="[22:51:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">indeed</span></div><div class="line" id="225132"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225132" label="[22:51:32]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps a bit more than minimal</span></div><div class="line" id="225143"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225143" label="[22:51:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we could probably do without unions in cc_x86 even though we have them</span></div><div class="line" id="225206"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225206" label="[22:52:06]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">My overarching goal here is making it so dozer can be compiled early on in the bootstrap chain</span></div><div class="line" id="225216"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225216" label="[22:52:16]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Right now it can be compiled by TinyCC, sort of</span></div><div class="line" id="225235"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225235" label="[22:52:35]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But I'd like to introduce it as early as I can</span></div><div class="line" id="225259"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225259" label="[22:52:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, the earliest possible stage is M2-Planet I think</span></div><div class="line" id="225304"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225304" label="[22:53:04]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That's what I thought</span></div><div class="line" id="225313"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225313" label="[22:53:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">cc_x86 is too limited</span></div><div class="line" id="225327"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225327" label="[22:53:27]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I originally wanted to do mescc but I don't know enough Scheme to effectively modify it to compile QBE</span></div><div class="line" id="225331"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225331" label="[22:53:31]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and anything earlier would required rust_x86  written in assembly :D</span></div><div class="line" id="225341"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225341" label="[22:53:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which is not an option I think</span></div><div class="line" id="225345"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225345" label="[22:53:45]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I would rather dips my toes in acid</span></div><div class="line" id="225358"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225358" label="[22:53:58]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, scheme tends to be hard for most people</span></div><div class="line" id="225404"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225404" label="[22:54:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I had to modify mescc a few times</span></div><div class="line" id="225407"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225407" label="[22:54:07]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">You can probably get some kind of &quot;proto-Rust&quot; written in Assembly, that supports structs and impls and stuff but not traits and generics</span></div><div class="line" id="225407"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225407" label="[22:54:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it's always hard for me</span></div><div class="line" id="225422"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225422" label="[22:54:22]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though some people like scheme a lot and find it easier</span></div><div class="line" id="225425"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225425" label="[22:54:25]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">But at that point you basically have C written with a different syntax, and at that point you may as well just use C</span></div><div class="line" id="225434"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225434" label="[22:54:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">exactly</span></div><div class="line" id="225442"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225442" label="[22:54:42]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Frankly it's less of Scheme itself and more of mes's codebase</span></div><div class="line" id="225454"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225454" label="[22:54:54]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">It's quite dense for a Scheme program</span></div><div class="line" id="225524"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225524" label="[22:55:24]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Actually, hmm</span></div><div class="line" id="225535"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225535" label="[22:55:35]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I wonder if it would be possible to make it so M2-Planet could compile TinyCC?</span></div><div class="line" id="225549"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225549" label="[22:55:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't see why not</span></div><div class="line" id="225617"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225617" label="[22:56:17]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Let me see what happens if I run M2-Planet on one of tcc's headers</span></div><div class="line" id="225655"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225655" label="[22:56:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though probably quite a bit of work requried</span></div><div class="line" id="225655"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225655" label="[22:56:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">perhaps not in terms of missing features</span></div><div class="line" id="225714"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225714" label="[22:57:14]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Right</span></div><div class="line" id="225723"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225723" label="[22:57:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but there is a lot of fiddling at the end when you are really close to bootstrapping tcc</span></div><div class="line" id="225728"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225728" label="[22:57:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and need to sort out various bugs</span></div><div class="line" id="225734"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225734" label="[22:57:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i.e. tcc depends on int being  4 bytes, etc...</span></div><div class="line" id="225736"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225736" label="[22:57:36]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Ah, it bails on a multi-line macro</span></div><div class="line" id="225808"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225808" label="[22:58:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, probably limitation of pre-processor</span></div><div class="line" id="225813"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225813" label="[22:58:13]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I figured</span></div><div class="line" id="225816"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225816" label="[22:58:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there is M2-Mesoplanet preprocessor</span></div><div class="line" id="225817"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225817" label="[22:58:17]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Let me try mesoplanet instead</span></div><div class="line" id="225817"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225817" label="[22:58:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that we have</span></div><div class="line" id="225821"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225821" label="[22:58:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is a bit more powerfull</span></div><div class="line" id="225830"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225830" label="[22:58:30]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">'unable to read file: &quot;config.h&quot;'</span></div><div class="line" id="225837"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225837" label="[22:58:37]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Is there a way to set include path in mesoplanet?</span></div><div class="line" id="225845"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225845" label="[22:58:45]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think so</span></div><div class="line" id="225852"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225852" label="[22:58:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">might be just -I but let me check</span></div><div class="line" id="225857"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225857" label="[22:58:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: do you remember?</span></div><div class="line" id="225933"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225933" label="[22:59:33]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">looks like -I should work</span></div><div class="line" id="225949"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#225949" label="[22:59:49]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">`./M2-Mesoplanet -f ~/Projects/TinyCC/tinycc/tcc.h -I ~/Projects/TinyCC/tinycc` fails with the same error</span></div><div class="line" id="225952"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#225952" label="[22:59:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Mesoplanet/blob/main/cc.c#L92">https://github.com/oriansj/M2-Mesoplanet/blob/main/cc.c#L92</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="230010"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230010" label="[23:00:10]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I see that line, it sets &quot;M2LIBC_PATH&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="230019"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230019" label="[23:00:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's path to M2libc directory...</span></div><div class="line" id="230055"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230055" label="[23:00:55]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe it doesn't accept any other include dirs</span></div><div class="line" id="230102"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230102" label="[23:01:02]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">just allows searcdhing for M2libc</span></div><div class="line" id="230103"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230103" label="[23:01:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm</span></div><div class="line" id="230116"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230116" label="[23:01:16]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I ran with the PWD set to tinycc's dir, and it looks like it fails when it tries to read in stdarg.h</span></div><div class="line" id="230141"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230141" label="[23:01:41]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">So it looks like stdarg.h would need to be added to M2libc</span></div><div class="line" id="230157"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230157" label="[23:01:57]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">...which means vararg support would need to be added to M2-Planet</span></div><div class="line" id="230202"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230202" label="[23:02:02]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*le sigh*</span></div><div class="line" id="230210"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230210" label="[23:02:10]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmm, do we have them in mescc</span></div><div class="line" id="230223"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230223" label="[23:02:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, even mescc only builds patched tcc</span></div><div class="line" id="230230"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230230" label="[23:02:30]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though I think it does have varargs...</span></div><div class="line" id="230329"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230329" label="[23:03:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ekaitz and I had some issues with varargs when bootstrapping tcc from mescc on riscv64...</span></div><div class="line" id="230330"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230330" label="[23:03:30]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm assuming this would be an issue in building QBE as well, since QBE has vararg functions</span></div><div class="line" id="230342"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230342" label="[23:03:42]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, so that's one of the missing features...</span></div><div class="line" id="230345"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#230345" label="[23:03:45]">&lt;ekaitz&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: didn't we fix those?</span></div><div class="line" id="230349"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230349" label="[23:03:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ekaitz: in mescc</span></div><div class="line" id="230353"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230353" label="[23:03:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">this is M2-Planet</span></div><div class="line" id="230356"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#230356" label="[23:03:56]">&lt;ekaitz&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh</span></div><div class="line" id="230406"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230406" label="[23:04:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">M2-Planet doesn't seem to have any varargs</span></div><div class="line" id="230423"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230423" label="[23:04:23]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">probably another missing feature would be support for &quot;i++ or ++i&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="230443"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230443" label="[23:04:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">M2-Planet doesn't support pre or post inc/de-crement operators</span></div><div class="line" id="230457"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230457" label="[23:04:57]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Right, I think QBE uses those too</span></div><div class="line" id="230513"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230513" label="[23:05:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">type sizes might be an issue too as I've mentioned</span></div><div class="line" id="230552"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230552" label="[23:05:52]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Mmm</span></div><div class="line" id="230559"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230559" label="[23:05:59]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so it's still quite a bit of work...</span></div><div class="line" id="230613"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230613" label="[23:06:13]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">even though M2-Planet is not that far behind mescc</span></div><div class="line" id="230616"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230616" label="[23:06:16]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah I think my plan is probably just going to be to use TinyCC as my bootstrap base for now</span></div><div class="line" id="230624"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230624" label="[23:06:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, that's a good start anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="230640"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230640" label="[23:06:40]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Then once dozer is in a more manageable state and I can bootstrap rustc v1 from there, I'll go back and see if I can make any progress from the other end</span></div><div class="line" id="230748"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230748" label="[23:07:48]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tcc is still relatively early</span></div><div class="line" id="230824"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230824" label="[23:08:24]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Mmm yeah, and even then it takes a bit of work to get it working with QBE</span></div><div class="line" id="230847"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230847" label="[23:08:47]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, QBE was mentioned in this channel before</span></div><div class="line" id="230850"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230850" label="[23:08:50]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">like <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/notgull/dozer/src/branch/main/tools/tinycc_qbe_conv.c">https://codeberg.org/notgull/dozer/src/branch/main/tools/tinycc_qbe_conv.c</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="230853"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230853" label="[23:08:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but nobody tried it from M2-Planet...</span></div><div class="line" id="230919"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230919" label="[23:09:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh you mean using tcc with qbe</span></div><div class="line" id="230946"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230946" label="[23:09:46]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah tinycc can compile qbe relatively easily, but it can't handle QBE's assembly syntax</span></div><div class="line" id="230949"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#230949" label="[23:09:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, tcc assembly support is not too good</span></div><div class="line" id="230955"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#230955" label="[23:09:55]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I think one of the Hare guys actually proposed a patch to tcc to fix that</span></div><div class="line" id="231007"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231007" label="[23:10:07]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which I think is because tcc is not a C to assembly compiler...</span></div><div class="line" id="231021"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231021" label="[23:10:21]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah here <a rel="nofollow" href="https://lists.nongnu.org/archive/html/tinycc-devel/2024-09/msg00005.html">https://lists.nongnu.org/archive/html/tinycc-devel/2024-09/msg00005.html</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="231025"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231025" label="[23:10:25]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it is directly converting C to binary code</span></div><div class="line" id="231053"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231053" label="[23:10:53]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, they should just push that patch to tcc</span></div><div class="line" id="231057"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231057" label="[23:10:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if there were no objections</span></div><div class="line" id="231105"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231105" label="[23:11:05]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Can you just, do that?</span></div><div class="line" id="231106"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231106" label="[23:11:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tcc mob branch is open to pushes</span></div><div class="line" id="231109"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231109" label="[23:11:09]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, anybody can push</span></div><div class="line" id="231114"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231114" label="[23:11:14]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Huh, let me try pushing it</span></div><div class="line" id="231151"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231151" label="[23:11:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there are instructions here <a rel="nofollow" href="https://repo.or.cz/tinycc.git">https://repo.or.cz/tinycc.git</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="231218"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231218" label="[23:12:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(just basic add remote, fetch, push...)</span></div><div class="line" id="231247"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231247" label="[23:12:47]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Yeah it's been a month or so, I'll just push it...</span></div><div class="line" id="231327"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231327" label="[23:13:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though don't hold your breath about it ending up in release</span></div><div class="line" id="231333"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231333" label="[23:13:33]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there are calls for a new release there</span></div><div class="line" id="231340"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231340" label="[23:13:40]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but it has been now maybe 8 years...</span></div><div class="line" id="231350"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231350" label="[23:13:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok, just 6...</span></div><div class="line" id="231350"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231350" label="[23:13:50]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Mmm</span></div><div class="line" id="231351"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231351" label="[23:13:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but still</span></div><div class="line" id="231403"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#231403" label="[23:14:03]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Who's even in charge of that these days?</span></div><div class="line" id="231417"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#231417" label="[23:14:17]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">grischka I think is the maintainer</span></div><div class="line" id="233150"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#233150" label="[23:31:50]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">notgull: I'd suggest not using molassescc, I mean, mescc, for anything more than necessary</span></div><div class="line" id="233248"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#233248" label="[23:32:48]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">compiling tcc with mescc is the single slowest step of the early bootstrap, before we have a real Linux kernel - by comparison, the next set of recompiling tcc with itself is virtually instantaneous</span></div><div class="line" id="233429"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#233429" label="[23:34:29]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">compiling Dozer with mescc would probably take weeks even on a Zen 5 CPU</span></div><div class="line" id="233718"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#233718" label="[23:37:18]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I've pushed the TinyCC patch now</span></div><div class="line" id="234002"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#234002" label="[23:40:02]">&lt;notgull&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: Yeah makes sense</span></div><br /></div></body></html>